nt is permitted or
            required to hold. Each attribute appears as a pair of
            attribute name and attribute value (known as a key-value
            pair). Many attributes are used for several different
            elements in LIDO. For many of the LIDO attributes there are
            recommended value lists provided by the LIDO Terminology.
            This particularly applies to the <a
            href="http://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#type"><em>lido:type</em></a>
            attribute.</p>
            <p>Fourteen attributes are defined in the LIDO Schema
            Version 1.1 namespace. In addition, the language attribute
            <a
            href="https://www.w3.org/TR/REC-xml/#sec-lang-tag"><em>xml:lang</em></a>
            from the XML namespace can be used for all text elements and
            is mandatory for the elements &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#administrativeMetadata">lido:administrativeMetadata</a>&gt;
            and &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#descriptiveMetadata">lido:descriptiveMetadata</a>&gt;.</p>
            <h3 data-number="5.4.1" id="type"><span
            class="header-section-number">5.4.1</span> <span
            class="citation" data-cites="type">@type</span></h3>
            <p><strong>The type attribute</strong> is of particular
            importance. The <a href="#content-lido-terminology">LIDO
            Terminology</a> contains LIDO Vocabularies for some type
            attributes, depending on the element in which the attribute
            occurs:</p>
            <ul>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/actor_type">Actor
            Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/classification_type">Classification
            Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/earliestDate_type">Earliest
            Date Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/eventPlace_type">Event
            Place Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/genderActor_type">Gender
            Actor Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/identifier_type">Identifier
            Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/latestDate_type">Latest
            Date Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/nationalityActor_type">Nationality
            Actor Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/objectDescriptionSet_type">Object
            Description Set Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/objectMeasurementsSet_type">Object
            Measurements Set Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/objectWorkType_type">Object/Work
            Type Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/periodName_type">Period
            Name Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/recordInfoSet_type">Record
            Info Set Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/recordMetadataDate_type">Record
            Metadata Date Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/repositorySet_type">Repository
            Set Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/workID_type">Repository
            Work ID Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/resourceRepresentation_type">Resource
            Representation Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/rightsType_type">Rights
            Type Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/termMaterialsTech_type">Term
            Materials/Techniques Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/vitalDatesActor_type">Vital
            Dates Actor Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            <li><a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/vitalPlaceActor_type">Vital
            Place Actor Type-Vocabulary</a></li>
            </ul>
            <h3 data-number="5.4.2" id="addedsearchterm"><span
            class="header-section-number">5.4.2</span> <span
            class="citation"
            data-cites="addedSearchTerm">@addedSearchTerm</span></h3>
            <p><strong>Attribute name:</strong> <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#addedSearchTerm">lido:addedSearchTerm</a></p>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> Boolean attribute of the &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#term">lido:term</a>&gt;
            element indicating that the term has been added to enhance
            retrieval when set to “yes” (“no” is default). The
            additional term may be a synonym, a broader term, or an
            equivalent term in a further language, taken from a local or
            published controlled vocabulary.</p>
            <p>Possible use cases for adding a term are: The index term
            from a linked data vocabulary (a) does not provide a
            (correct) term in the desired language; (b) does not include
            a synonym considered useful as search entry by the data
            provider; (c) is not expected to be fully exploited with
            regard to its semantic relationships by the portal, so that
            adding a broader term is useful for expanding the search
            result.</p>
            <p><strong>Example 1:</strong> The following XML code
            snippet is an example for use case (a): The <a
            href="http://vocab.getty.edu/page/aat/300015050">AAT
            descriptor</a> provides an incorrect German equivalent,
            ‘Ölgemälde’.</p>
            <div class="sourceCode" id="cb46"><pre
            class="sourceCode xml"><code class="sourceCode xml"><span id="cb46-1"><a href="#cb46-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="co">&lt;!-- Medium --&gt;</span></span>
<span id="cb46-2"><a href="#cb46-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>&lt;<span class="kw">lido:termMaterialsTech</span></span>
<span id="cb46-3"><a href="#cb46-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="ot">   lido:type=</span><span class="st">&quot;http://terminology.lido-schema.org/lido00513&quot;</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb46-4"><a href="#cb46-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>   &lt;<span class="kw">skos:Concept</span></span>
<span id="cb46-5"><a href="#cb46-5" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="ot">       rdf:about=</span><span class="st">&quot;http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300015050&quot;</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb46-6"><a href="#cb46-6" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>       &lt;<span class="kw">skos:prefLabel</span></span>
<span id="cb46-7"><a href="#cb46-7" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="ot">           xml:lang=</span><span class="st">&quot;en&quot;</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb46-8"><a href="#cb46-8" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>           oil paint (paint)</span>
<span id="cb46-9"><a href="#cb46-9" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>       &lt;/<span class="kw">skos:prefLabel</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb46-10"><a href="#cb46-10" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>       <span class="co">&lt;!-- The following label is an incorrect German translation --&gt;</span></span>
<span id="cb46-11"